# Copy to `.env` at the repo root (backend/.env also works and wins).
# Production values live in the Railway dashboard (deployment.md), never in a file.

# Matches docker-compose.yml for local dev.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://waypoint:waypoint@localhost:5432/waypoint

# Signs access JWTs and invite tokens (ADR-0020). Generate: openssl rand -base64 32
JWT_SECRET=

# Encrypts Google refresh tokens at rest (ADR-0020); must be base64 decoding to
# exactly 32 bytes. Generate: openssl rand -base64 32
TOKEN_ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# Google OAuth client (prerequisites-checklist.md).
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=
GOOGLE_OAUTH_REDIRECT_URI=http://localhost:3000/auth/google/callback

# Google Maps & Places server key (ADR-0108) — backend-only, held by the Places
# proxy for Places API (New) + Routes; NEVER exposed to the browser, NEVER a VITE_
# var. Required in production (the picker proxy reads it via requireEnv); a
# dev/DEV_AUTH box may omit it (only the search/resolve routes 500 without it).
# GOOGLE_MAPS_SERVER_KEY=

# Per-member·trip rate limits on the paid Places proxy routes (ADR-0108 §5).
# Optional — these are the built-in defaults, override only to tune. Two windows
# each: a per-minute burst cap and a per-day drip cap.
# PLACES_SEARCH_LIMIT_PER_MIN=120
# PLACES_SEARCH_LIMIT_PER_DAY=2000
# PLACES_RESOLVE_LIMIT_PER_MIN=30
# PLACES_RESOLVE_LIMIT_PER_DAY=500

# Document encryption at rest (ADR-0015). Must decode to exactly 32 bytes.
# Generate: openssl rand -base64 32
DOC_ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# Railway Storage Bucket (S3-compatible, ADR-0031). Leave unset locally — documents
# fall back to ./backend/storage/documents (gitignored).
# S3_ENDPOINT=
# S3_BUCKET=
# S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=

# Place enrichment (ADR-0166). Ships ENABLED; set this to any value to stop every
# outbound pass — it is the one switch that stops this app talking to third parties on
# its own initiative (§14). Reads are unaffected: they serve already-stored data.
# ENRICHMENT_DISABLED=1

# Web Push (ADR-0197). This server's identity to every push service: the public half is
# handed to the browser at subscribe time and rides `/me`, the private half signs each
# request and never leaves the process. Generate BOTH with:
#
#   npx web-push generate-vapid-keys
#
# Required in production; a dev box may omit all three, and then `/me` reports no key and
# the settings surface says so rather than offering a control that cannot work. They are
# checked as a SET at boot — half a keypair subscribes fine and fails at the first send —
# and the two are size-checked (65 bytes public, 32 private) so a copy-paste that swaps
# them is caught then rather than at the first send.
# VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=
# VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=
# A mailto: or https: URL the push services can use to reach whoever runs this deployment.
# VAPID_SUBJECT=mailto:you@example.com

# Ships ENABLED once the keys are set. **Set it to `1` to stop every send, and DELETE the
# line to re-enable — never set `0` or `false`.** It is a bare truthiness check on the
# string, and in JavaScript `"0"` and `"false"` are both truthy, so `PUSH_DISABLED=false`
# stops every push. Only an absent or empty value re-enables. (Same shape as
# ENRICHMENT_DISABLED; note FX_DISABLED differs, testing `=== '1'`.)
#
# It exists because this is a thing the app does on its own initiative. Subscribing,
# unsubscribing and every in-app surface keep working — those are the primary, and push is
# the amplifier.
# PUSH_DISABLED=1

# ── Routing (ADR-0205) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Where the routing engine lives. Unset uses the FOSSGIS planet server, which ADR-0205 §Y1
# keeps as the standing default — set this only to point at a self-host.
#
# **`valhalla1` is the API host.** `https://valhalla.openstreetmap.de/` is the demo WEB APP:
# it answers 200 with an HTML page for every API path, so a deploy pointed there does not
# fail, it silently returns nothing forever (§Z4). The backend refuses to start on a value
# that is not a bare https origin whose host the outbound allowlist carries — and that
# allowlist is code (`enrichment/outbound-fetch.ts`), so a self-host is two lines, not one.
# ROUTING_BASE_URL=https://valhalla1.openstreetmap.de

# Per-request timeout for a matrix or a shape (default 15000). A day matrix measures
# ~560 ms median / ~1 s tail, and nothing user-facing waits on it — the warm runs behind a
# 202 — so this bounds a hung socket rather than a slow answer.
# ROUTING_FETCH_TIMEOUT_MS=15000

# Kill switch for outbound routing. Reads are unaffected and that is the point: a route is a
# cache, so flipping this serves every leg already stored and lets the rest read as the
# crow-flies chip (ADR-0206 §D4). The endpoint still answers.
# ROUTING_DISABLED=1

# The app's own origin. Locally that's the Vite dev server (the CORS origin for the
# :5173 → :3000 gap); in a DEPLOYED environment it is that environment's canonical
# host — the post-login redirect target, and what every other host answering for the
# service is redirected to (ADR-0169). Required in production, where it must name the
# same host as GOOGLE_OAUTH_REDIRECT_URI or the backend refuses to start.
FRONTEND_URL=http://localhost:5173

# ── Dev-only. NEVER set this in production. ─────────────────────────────────
# Uncomment to bypass Google login locally. It also gates POST /notifications/test,
# which sends a real push to every device the caller has registered — the proof that the
# pipe works end to end, and the only send that exists before the sweep (ADR-0197 §3).
# DEV_AUTH=1

# Mounts the push instrument in user settings (a subscribe/unsubscribe gesture and this
# device's endpoint). A build-time frontend flag, so it belongs in `frontend/.env` — and
# it must be set for a PRODUCTION build, because there is no service worker under
# `pnpm dev` and therefore no push to test:
#
#   VITE_PUSH_DEBUG=1 pnpm --filter @waypoint/frontend build
#
# VITE_PUSH_DEBUG=1

# ── Itinerary PDF (ADR-0213 §4) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# All optional. The production image installs chromium at the default path and these are
# the shipped values; set them only to point at a different browser or to re-tune the
# render bound. PDF_RENDER_CONCURRENCY is the one that matters under load: each render is a
# real browser tab, and the route is public, so an unbounded value is a memory lever anyone
# with a link can pull. Work queued behind it answers 503 after PDF_RENDER_TIMEOUT_MS.
# PDF_CHROMIUM_PATH=/usr/bin/chromium
# PDF_RENDER_TIMEOUT_MS=15000
# PDF_RENDER_CONCURRENCY=2
